<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yangtze River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yangtze</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yangtze is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, flowing from the Tibetan plateau east to the sea at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>. Its basin became China's economic heartland, producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> and other goods. It served as the principal commercial artery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>, the defensive barrier of the Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>, and the base from which the Ming founder rose to power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yangtze-river.jpg" alt="Yangtze River" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Hitchcock · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dusk_on_the_Yangtze_River.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Eastern Jin was the continuation, south of the <b>Yangtze</b>, of the Western Jin dynasty founded by the Sima family in 266.</blockquote><a href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Agriculture was the basis of the economy: millet, wheat, and rice grown across the Yellow and <b>Yangtze River</b> valleys produced the surplus that funded the imperial state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Jin forces pursued the new Southern Song court south of the <b>Yangtze</b> in 1129–1130 but were checked by Song generals such as Han Shizhong and Yue Fei, and the Jin governed part of the conquered land through the puppet state of Qi from 1130 to 1137.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Control of the <b>Yangtze</b> and Yellow River basins, together with the support of leading regional elites, enabled the Ming to stabilise China and begin a period of growth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When the civil war resumed in 1946, the People’s Liberation Army won the decisive Liaoshen, Huaihai and Pingjin campaigns of 1948–49 and crossed the <b>Yangtze</b> in April 1949.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Yangtze River</b> system and the Grand Canal were the main routes, carrying grain, silk, cotton, porcelain and tea between producing regions and consuming centres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
